# SelectoUpdato Portable System Guide

This Livebook demonstrates the current architecture: the domain governs write
intent, SelectoUpdato validates and compiles it, and the configured Selecto
database adapter owns preview and execution.

There is no Ecto repository, schema, changeset, or application Ecto
configuration in this flow.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Domain["Selecto domain writes"] --> Updato["SelectoUpdato intent compiler"]
  Updato --> Command["Selecto.Write.Command"]
  Command --> Selecto["Configured Selecto"]
  Selecto --> Adapter["Database write adapter"]
```

## Install local packages

```elixir
find_local_package = fn package ->
  [
    Path.expand("../#{package}", __DIR__),
    Path.expand("../../#{package}", __DIR__),
    Path.expand("../../../#{package}", __DIR__)
  ]
  |> Enum.find(&File.exists?(Path.join(&1, "mix.exs")))
end

selecto_path = find_local_package.("selecto")

Mix.install([
  if(selecto_path, do: {:selecto, path: selecto_path}, else: {:selecto, "~> 0.5.0"})
])
```

## A database-neutral demonstration adapter

Real applications select an adapter package such as the PostgreSQL, SQLite,
MySQL, or SQL Server Selecto adapter. This small in-memory adapter makes the
ownership boundary visible without requiring any database or Ecto setup.

```elixir
defmodule PortableGuide.Adapter do
  @behaviour Selecto.DB.Adapter
  @behaviour Selecto.DB.WriteAdapter

  def name, do: :portable_guide
  def connect(connection), do: {:ok, connection}
  def execute(_connection, _query, _params, _opts), do: {:ok, %{rows: [], columns: []}}
  def placeholder(index), do: ["$", Integer.to_string(index)]
  def quote_identifier(identifier), do: [34, to_string(identifier), 34]
  def supports?(_feature), do: false

  def write_capabilities(_connection) do
    %{
      protocol_version: Selecto.Write.Capabilities.protocol_version(),
      insert: true,
      update: true,
      upsert: true,
      delete: true,
      returning: true,
      transactions: true
    }
  end

  def preview_write(_connection, command, _opts) do
    {:ok, %Selecto.Write.Preview{statements: [], metadata: %{command: command}}}
  end

  # Writes arrive only through SelectoUpdato, with an authorization for
  # exactly this command. A real adapter refuses anything else.
  def execute_write(_connection, command, opts) do
    with :ok <- Selecto.Write.Authorization.require_for(command, opts) do
      {:ok,
       %Selecto.Write.Result{
         operation: command.operation,
         affected_rows: 1,
         rows: [],
         metadata: %{adapter: :portable_guide}
       }}
    end
  end

  def transaction(_connection, fun, _opts), do: fun.(:portable_guide)
end
```

## The domain is the write authority

The domain declares writable fields, mandatory tenant scope, and operation
cardinality. Neither the UI nor the database adapter is allowed to invent
these rules.

```elixir
domain = %{
  name: "Portable work items",
  source: %{
    source_table: "work_items",
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :tenant_id, :title, :state],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer},
      tenant_id: %{type: :integer},
      title: %{type: :string},
      state: %{type: :string}
    },
    associations: %{}
  },
  schemas: %{},
  joins: %{},
  writes: %{
    scope: %{tenant: %{required: true, field: :tenant_id}},
    operations: %{
      insert: %{enabled: true, expected_cardinality: {:exactly, 1}},
      update: %{enabled: true, expected_cardinality: {:exactly, 1}}
    },
    fields: %{
      id: %{insertable: false, updatable: false},
      tenant_id: %{insertable: true, updatable: false, required_on: [:insert]},
      title: %{insertable: true, updatable: true, required_on: [:insert]},
      state: %{insertable: true, updatable: true, required_on: [:insert]}
    }
  }
}

:ok = SelectoUpdato.validate_domain(domain)
```

## Compile and preview a governed update

The required tenant filter is expressed in the portable command. The adapter
receives the command only after SelectoUpdato has enforced the domain contract.

```elixir
selecto = %Selecto{
  adapter: PortableGuide.Adapter,
  connection: :portable_guide,
  domain: domain
}

operation =
  domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new()
  |> SelectoUpdato.with_tenant(%{tenant_id: 45})
  |> SelectoUpdato.filter({"id", 9001})
  |> SelectoUpdato.update(%{"state" => "done"})
  |> SelectoUpdato.expected_cardinality({:exactly, 1})
  |> SelectoUpdato.returning(:all)

{:ok, command} = SelectoUpdato.Executor.build_write_plan(operation, selecto)
{:ok, preview} = SelectoUpdato.preview(operation, selecto)

%{
  operation: command.operation,
  predicate: command.predicate,
  expected_cardinality: command.expected_cardinality,
  preview_metadata: preview.metadata
}
```

## Execute through the same adapter boundary

```elixir
{:ok, result} = SelectoUpdato.execute(operation, selecto)

%{
  operation: result.operation,
  affected_rows: result.affected_rows,
  adapter: result.metadata.adapter
}
```

## Architecture rules

- SelectoUpdato contains no database-driver or Ecto dependency.
- Domains own writable fields, tenant filters, relationship sources,
  transition rules, and expected cardinality.
- `Selecto.Write.Command` is the portable handoff object.
- Adapter packages own SQL dialect, placeholders, transactions, capability
  discovery, preview, execution, and result normalization.
- Unsupported nested or collection writes fail closed until an adapter declares
  and implements the required capability.
